3","d4b1f7f1ab3cb5ea","d82f82ce2e296a0a","db23c50bd1a6a8c2","e3cd36a7ed426cb5","e4e150c65a87c77e","e924cd8af835664f","ed62eb2ec785e04f","edd1d645720b5ca4","ef390c6b7c843423","f1b4b553afca9371","f48eaca71d6b431e","f49ec636cf1add44","f558df7be9c000d0","f59a1c185889db7f","f7ba61f6a27cc9ca","f7cb63b879a73f7f","fd0572740da7f10f"],"groups":["203a3e63c791c6e4","94d20095687fedf1","196483d0de64c7c4","75e17bfa4b1df32d","5da3ca5b7d569e27","d66e21bb0df8deb4","42414ac49875a804","d43354e7d94e530c"],"findings":"## Review of the timing-guard changes\n\nI ran the ratio test and the catastrophic-regex guard. I did not run the browser tests, so those findings are from reading only.\n\n**1. Mismatches**\n\n- **Test names still say \"fast\" but the budgets are now 30–60 s (read).** `splitting a large new file stays fast` (budget 30 s, `budgets`) and `a review line of 10 MB is skipped with a warning, fast` (budget 30 s, `budgets`) now assert a slow-not-hang bound, not speed. The names overstate what the guards check.\n- **The TODO drops the Reviews-pane flake without saying it is fixed (read).** The `todo` group replaces the whole flaky-timing bullet. The Reviews-pane decisions test, which commit 2a096dd had just flagged as load-sensitive, is no longer tracked as open. The `wait-for` group implies it is covered (the 11 s failure), but nothing in the diff shows that test was run under load.\n- **Evidence for \"between loaded-good and attack\" is incomplete (read).** The `budgets` evidence lists only mutation times. There are no loaded-good times for the markdown, claims or 10 MB tests. For `claims html`, the attack takes 40 s against a 30 s budget, a margin of about 10 s with no measured loaded-good time behind it.\n\n**2. Misgraded necessity**\n\n- `budgets` (required) and `signals-ratio` (required) are fairly graded. The task asks for reliable guards, so the budget changes are the fix.\n- `wait-for` is labelled supporting and \"serves budgets\". It is the other half of the same fix, not a separate need, so `supporting` is a reasonable label but the `serves` link is loose.\n- `drop-hostile-timing` (incidental) is accurate. Removing the 3 s check was a deliberate choice to remove a flaky, unmutable assertion, which is what the task asked for.\n- `todo` (supporting) is documentation, not a dependency of the other groups. \"Supporting\" is defensible, but it is effectively mechanical.\n\n**3. Removal candidates**\n\nNone are clearly removable without loss. `time-helper` could be inlined at its 9 call sites, but it centralises the printing and the \"slow, not hang\" note, so keeping it is better.\n\n**4. Simplifications**\n\n- In `signals.test.ts`, the `assert.equal(analyze(...).hunks.length, ...)` sits inside the timed loop, adding to the measured time. Hoisting the check out of the loop, or checking once before timing, would make the measurement purer.\n- `waitFor` has a `probe` that can throw, and the caller does not distinguish that from \"not yet\" (see risk 1 below).\n\n**5. Missing risks**\n\n- **`waitFor` swallows every probe error (read).** Errors from `list()` and `evaluate()` are caught as \"not yet\". Before this change the tab-discovery loop let a `fetch` error propagate at once. Now a dead Chromium or a closed socket stalls for the full 30 s and then reports a generic \"timed out waiting for …\", hiding the real error. The `ready()` change makes this worse, because a broken page now costs 30 s per `ready` call (a 30 s Chromium start plus 30 s for each `ready`) before failing. The author's note covers \"stall up to 30 s\", but not the masked error or the cumulative time.\n- **The `wait-for` behaviour change is broader than the note says (read).** Every existing browser test that relied on `ready()` silently continuing now fails hard. That is intended, but it means a page that renders slowly but correctly can now fail where it previously passed through to its own asserts.\n- **Ratio test under load (verified for a clean run only).** I ran `performance: analyze` once on this machine: 100 hunks 15.8 ms, 900 hunks 146.9 ms, a ratio of **9.3x** against the 30x limit, which matches the author's \"good code 7–9x\". I did not measure it under load, so the claim \"2–9x loaded\" is unverified here. The best-of-five approach is reasonable, but a stall on the 900-hunk side can still inflate the ratio.\n- **Catastrophic-regex guard (verified).** `within('catastrophic regex', …)` ran at 450 ms against a 30 s budget and the test passed. Its functional asserts were not separately checked.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
