,"e4e150c65a87c77e","e924cd8af835664f","ed62eb2ec785e04f","f1b4b553afca9371","f48eaca71d6b431e","f59a1c185889db7f","f5ba9711b435c3a7","f7cb63b879a73f7f","fd0572740da7f10f"],"groups":["00ce6c9d78bbc000","3e640e8d19a52188","196483d0de64c7c4","1e25795f82b819b9","2f7d6a76e6bc579e","d66e21bb0df8deb4","8a05811e8c56426b","d43354e7d94e530c"],"findings":"The change has one minor weakness in the new signals performance test. I found nothing else worth fixing.\n\n**Minor: the `+ 300` slack weakens the signals ratio test** (`test/signals.test.ts`, the last assert of the \"performance\" test). **Verified** by running it once, not by simulating a regression.\n- The assert is `msBig <= 30 * msSmall + 300`. On this machine the 100-hunk run takes 23 ms and the 900-hunk run 167 ms (7.2x).\n- A quadratic scan would reach about 81 × 23 ≈ 1.9 s, and the limit is 30 × 23 + 300 ≈ 990 ms, so it would still be caught here.\n- On a faster machine, where the small run is about 5 ms, a quadratic regression would take about 405 ms against a limit of 450 ms, and it would pass. The 300 ms constant dominates the limit whenever `analyze` is fast, so the guard only works at the speed it was tuned on.\n- A tighter factor (about 20x) with a much smaller constant, or a larger input, would keep the test sensitive. This reintroduces some flake risk, which is the trade-off the author was avoiding.\n\nI also read the other changes and found no defects:\n- **`waitFor`:** errors from the probe are swallowed and retried, as before. The timeout message drops the last probe error, which only makes diagnosis harder.\n- **`until` in `ary.test.ts`:** it now throws `Error` instead of calling `assert.fail`, which has the same effect.\n- **Removed wall-time checks:** the checks were replaced with `within` budgets of 20 to 60 s, which only catch hangs. The tests' functional asserts are unchanged.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
